    "growth_divergence_warning": "Equity CAGR (1.7% 5yr) diverges far more than 30% from revenue (6.0%) and EPS (13.7%) CAGRs. Book value is meaningless as a growth proxy here: CME pays out ~100% of earnings via regular + annual variable dividends, so equity is flat by policy. Revenue 5yr CAGR is used instead; EPS CAGR is inflated by rate-driven collateral income (FY2020 near-zero rates -> FY2023-25 ~5%) and the FY2025 352.8 investment gain.",
    "notes": "Owner-FCF series = reported FCF - SBC - equity-award withholding taxes (corporate-cash interest not stripped historically; not separately disclosed per year). Owner-FCF CAGR is slightly ABOVE reported, so SBC is not outgrowing cash flow (SBC ~1.5-2% of revenue). Much of FY2022-25 FCF growth came from the rate cycle lifting collateral income from ~zero, which is not repeatable."
  },
  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "CME's benchmark rate (SOFR/Treasury) and equity-index futures franchise keeps its liquidity network effect; FY2026 revenue lands ~+7.5% (H1 +7.6%, July ADV +18%), then growth fades to ~5% as volumes normalise off records, with market data and new products (24/7 crypto, event contracts, FTSE Russell licence secured to 2037) offsetting modest rate-per-contract erosion. The Fed eases gradually, so the collateral spread (~$540m) shrinks in dollars and from ~7.7% to ~5.4% of revenue; operating margin creeps up on the ~$1.7bn expense guide. The Duffy->Fitzpatrick handover (Mar-2027) is internal and uneventful.",
    "bull": "Rates stay high and volatile and fiscal/term-premium uncertainty keeps rate futures and Treasury hedging at record volumes; cleared-collateral balances keep growing so the collateral spread holds its dollars; crypto 24/7, event contracts and Google Cloud tokenised collateral scale into meaningful lines, and market data keeps compounding double digits. Revenue grows 7-9% early, operating margin approaches 75%.",
    "bear": "Volume is cyclical to rate and macro volatility: a Fed cutting cycle both compresses rate-futures activity and roughly halves the collateral spread. Competition bites at the margin -- Cboe's FTSE Russell options licence, ICE's MSCI/FTSE futures push, and Kalshi/Polymarket/DraftKings capturing event-contract volume outside CME -- while volume tiers erode rate per contract. FY2027 revenue is roughly flat after a strong FY2026 and long-run growth settles ~3%, all-in margin down ~5pp, and a lower exit multiple as the market re-rates CME as ex-growth."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "CME is valued as a capital-light operating franchise on owner FCF, with the rate-sensitive collateral spread and the S&P DJI stake carried inside the flows rather than as separate balance-sheet assets, and amortization of acquired intangibles given no perpetual tax shield. At a 7.5% WACC the terminal cap binds in base and bull, so the value is largely a function of the exit multiple assumed on a ~3% grower.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Collateral-income sensitivity to the Fed path: ~7-8% of revenue-equivalent that could halve in a cutting cycle",
      "Volume normalisation from record FY2025-26 levels (base year may be near a cyclical peak)",
      "Competitive encroachment in equity index (Cboe, ICE) and event contracts (Kalshi, Polymarket)",
      "Terminal value dominance: the 20x cap on a ~3% terminal growth binds; a lower exit multiple moves value roughly one-for-one",
      "CEO transition March 2027"
    ]
  },
